// Local, uncommitted build settings for running DefAIke on your own device.
//
// `Scripts/generate-xcode-project.sh` copies this file to `Local.xcconfig` if that
// file does not exist yet. `Local.xcconfig` is gitignored; this example is not.
// Edit the copy, never this file.
//
// Why a separate file rather than Xcode's Signing & Capabilities tab: the
// `.xcodeproj` is generated from `project.yml` and is not committed, so anything
// you set in Xcode's UI is discarded the next time the project is regenerated.
// Values here survive regeneration.
//
// Nothing here is a release value. The Release Process supplies the distributed
// build identity and signing policy as approved artifacts; every setting below is
// empty or a development default, and a Release build that read one of these as a
// release claim would be wrong. `MainAppReleaseProvisioning` reads none of them.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Your Apple Developer team identifier.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Xcode → Settings → Accounts → select your Apple ID → the team list shows the
// ten-character Team ID. A free Apple ID appears as "Personal Team" and has one.
//
// Leave empty to keep the current behaviour: builds work for the Simulator and
// cannot be installed on a physical device.
DEFAIKE_LOCAL_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Bundle identifier prefix.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The app becomes `<prefix>.app`, the Share Extension `<prefix>.app.share`, and
// the shared App Group `group.<prefix>.app`. Changing the prefix changes all
// three together, which matters: the app and the extension must name the same
// App Group or the consented handoff has no container to meet in.
//
// Change it if `dev.defaike` collides with something already provisioned to your
// account. A reverse-DNS name you control is the convention.
//
// One consequence, so it is not a surprise: `check-capability-composition.py` and
// the release-archive audits identify an archive by its `CFBundleIdentifier` and
// compare it against their composition table, which names `dev.defaike.app`. A
// locally re-prefixed build fails that comparison. That is the audit doing its
// job — it exists so a bundle built from some other spec is not silently audited
// under this composition's rules — and it is not a reason to avoid changing this.
DEFAIKE_LOCAL_BUNDLE_PREFIX = dev.defaike
